#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1127
msgid "How a dictionary works: hashing"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1129
msgid "Why is `d[key]` fast, and why must keys not change?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1131
msgid "A dictionary keeps its pairs in a table of slots. To store a key, Python computes `hash(key)` - a big integer made from the value - and uses it to choose the slot. To find the key later, it computes the hash again and looks **in that slot only**. So a lookup costs the same for 10 keys or 10 million keys:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1142
msgid "A list is not hashable because it can **change**: after a change, its hash would change, and the dictionary would look in the wrong slot. That is why keys must be immutable values."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1146
msgid "The hash of a string changes **between runs** of Python (a security feature), but not during one run. Never save hashes to a file."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1149
msgid "The speed, measured:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1169
msgid "**Exercise**"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1171
msgid "Why is `{(1, [2, 3]): \"x\"}` an error, but `{(1, (2, 3)): \"x\"}` is fine?"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/06-dictionaries.md:1174
msgid "A tuple is hashable only if everything inside it is hashable. `(1, [2, 3])` contains a list, which can change, so the tuple has no hash. `(1, (2, 3))` contains only immutable values."
msgstr ""

